WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.842
Compare identity-based access lists with authority
carried in a capability.

00:00:04.842 --> 00:00:10.266
Each can express useful permissions, but delegation
and revocation work differently.

00:00:10.266 --> 00:00:15.495
Mandatory controls impose centrally managed
restrictions beyond owner discretion.

00:00:15.495 --> 00:00:21.629
Use the event-entry and food-coupon example to see
how one identity check can lead to a limited

00:00:21.629 --> 00:00:25.180
capability without making the two mechanisms
identical.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.574
A valid credential or signature does not settle
every trust question.

00:00:29.574 --> 00:00:35.153
Ask whether a message is fresh, intended for this
service and permitted for this action.

00:00:35.153 --> 00:00:38.576
Replay and compromised keys expose these
distinctions.

00:00:38.576 --> 00:00:44.789
Reputation can help assess expected behavior, but it
is contextual evidence rather than an access-

00:00:44.789 --> 00:00:48.339
control mechanism or a guarantee about the next
request.

00:00:48.339 --> 00:00:52.778
Browser security demonstrates defense in depth at
architectural scale.

00:00:52.778 --> 00:00:58.865
Restrict untrusted rendering, mediate privileged
operations, patch promptly and warn about known

00:00:58.865 --> 00:00:59.372
threats.

00:00:59.372 --> 00:01:04.444
These controls reduce different risks and still
matter when another layer fails.

00:01:04.444 --> 00:01:10.404
Treat the supplied Chrome diagrams as a historical
teaching case, and distinguish that durable

00:01:10.404 --> 00:01:14.145
reasoning from details that change across browser
versions.
